On easy problems search with the small planner is the better buy: with 4 samples' worth of compute it succeeds 0\.92 of the time, against 0\.74 for the big planner's single sample (@fig-search). On hard problems the big planner wins at every budget: at 64 it reaches 0\.64 against 0\.23, because the small planner's samples rarely land in the gap at all. And the verifier matters as much as the planner: a learned verifier that misjudges where the gap is levels off at 0\.954 on easy problems however many samples it gets, the chance that its own belief lies inside the gap.

### Why reasoning tokens add computation {#depth-intuition}

A transformer's forward pass has a fixed depth: one pass can do only a fixed number of sequential steps, set by its layers. Each generated token, though, is fed back in and gets another full pass. So writing $T$ intermediate tokens gives the model about $T$ times more sequential computation, and Li et al. proved that with $T$ reasoning tokens a constant-depth transformer can solve any problem a circuit of size $T$ solves, which without them it cannot [▶ 33:22](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2002). This is the formal side of explanation 3; that thinking tokens did not help robots suggests the robot policies were not short of computation, but short of the right features.

### Learning to reason with RL {#rl-intuition}

Chain-of-thought started as a prompting trick: show the model a few worked examples with their reasoning written out, and it writes reasoning for new questions [▶ 34:54](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2094). It only worked in large models, somewhere around 100 billion parameters; in small ones it hurt [▶ 35:26](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2126). And it is brittle: sensitive to the chosen examples, and limited to their style [▶ 37:00](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2220). Can a model instead be *trained* to reason [▶ 38:06](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2286)?

The answer is reinforcement learning, today usually **GRPO** [▶ 40:10](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2410). PPO needs a critic, a second network that predicts the return of every partial answer, which is expensive and hard to train for language [▶ 40:44](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2444). GRPO drops it: sample a group of answers to the same question, score each with a rule ("is the final answer right?"), and use each answer's reward relative to the group as its advantage [▶ 41:15](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2475). No human labels, no reward model, no value function; reasoning emerges because thinking carefully is the only reliable way to get the reward [▶ 41:47](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2507). DeepSeek-R1's model learned to say "wait" and reconsider, a word it almost never used before a point in training [▶ 43:55](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2635).

Why did this not work years earlier? Because RL can only reinforce what the model sometimes does: a base model that never produces a correct answer gives nothing to reinforce [▶ 42:53](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2573). The third toy shows both effects. A policy chooses how many reasoning steps to take; an answer is right (with chance 0\.9) only if it took at least 1 \(easy\)\, 4 \(medium\)\, 8 \(hard\) steps. It starts out preferring short answers and is trained by GRPO with groups of 8.

::: {#cell-fig-grpo .cell execution_count=7}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Third toy: GRPO on how many reasoning steps to take (mean over 8 seeds). Left: accuracy per kind of problem; right: mean number of steps taken. The starting policy prefers short answers (λ = 0.3).](w10-embodied-reasoning_files/figure-html/fig-grpo-output-1.png){#fig-grpo width=604 height=281 fig-alt='Two line charts sharing an x-axis of training steps from 0 to 300. Left, accuracy: easy (green) starts at about 0.66 and passes 0.85 after about 45 steps; medium (blue) starts at 0.25 and passes 0.85 after about 140; hard (orange) starts at 0.05, stays low for about 70 steps, then climbs steeply and passes 0.85 after about 190. All end near 0.89. Right, mean reasoning steps: all three start at about 2.5. Easy rises a little and ends near 2.6, medium rises to about 4.3 by step 80 and ends near 4.1, hard rises slowly and then steeply between steps 60 and 150, ending near 7.9.'}
