The boundary is sharp (@fig-coldstart). Every setting whose starting accuracy was 1\.65% or more learned within 300 steps in all 8 seeds; none at 1\.29% or less did, in any seed. A group of 8 answers then holds a right one only 10% of the time, and a group with no right answer has all its advantages equal to zero. That is the lecture's point about base models: RL amplifies a capability that is already there.

### From answers to grounded reasoning {#verify-intuition}

The same recipe works for vision: Visual-RFT applied GRPO with the overlap between a predicted and a true bounding box (IoU) as the verifiable reward [▶ 46:29](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2789). But rewarding only the final answer has a failure mode. In mathematics a right answer from wrong reasoning is rare; in vision, a model can describe a scene that is not there and still output the right box, by luck or by pattern matching, and get the full reward [▶ 48:33](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2913). ARGOS, the lecturer's work at Microsoft, also rewards the intermediate steps, checking with other models whether the objects and moments the reasoning refers to are really where it says [▶ 49:37](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=2977). On embodied planning benchmarks, reasoning trained this way did 3–4× better than supervised chain-of-thought on the hardest tasks [▶ 51:13](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=3073).

For continuous control, writing reasoning at every step stays too slow, so the picture the lecture ends with has two levels [▶ 52:15](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=3135) [▶ 52:46](https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE?t=3166):

