## Guest spotlight

**Archit Sharma** (research scientist at Google DeepMind working on post-training and reasoning, a creator of DPO, and lead of the Gemini Deep Think model that reached gold-medal level on International Mathematical Olympiad problems) spoke about scaling test-time compute at the frontier. Until 2024, most of the progress of language models came from scaling pre-training: ever larger models trained to predict the next token [▶ 01:35](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=95). From late 2024, **reasoning models** added a second axis: instead of answering at once, they think first, and accuracy rises predictably with the time allowed. In the Gemini 2.5 report, performance on the AIME maths competition rose by more than 20 points with thinking time [▶ 03:40](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=220). A system with test-time compute is one that, instead of a single fixed forward pass, searches in some way before answering [▶ 05:13](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=313); AlphaGo, whose search capped at 5 seconds per move took the same network from about 3,000 to about 5,000 Elo, is the classic case [▶ 06:15](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=375).

For language, chain-of-thought prompting showed the effect; RL with verifiable rewards made it a training method, and the length of the model's answers grows by itself over training [▶ 09:51](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=591). Longer chains are only one way to spend compute: sampling in parallel, revising previous answers, calling tools such as search or code, and verifying one's own answer are others, and agentic coding and research tools combine them in harnesses around a model [▶ 11:56](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=716). His example was Deep Think on olympiad proofs: with inference compute on a log scale, the share of proofs judged correct by experts rose from about 40% to about 90%, with the model working for hours [▶ 14:02](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=842). The gains extend to tasks without a verifier, such as generating a detailed 3-D scene in HTML [▶ 15:38](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=938).

Open questions he named: using tokens and tools efficiently [▶ 18:46](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1126); test-time scaling viewed as increasing the depth of computation, and whether tokens are the best way to add depth [▶ 19:19](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1159); keeping agents productive and safe over days or weeks [▶ 19:49](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1189); discovering which problems matter, not just solving well-posed ones [▶ 21:55](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1315); and improving with experience instead of reasoning from first principles every time [▶ 22:58](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1378). In the Q&A: reasoning in continuous latent space could be more efficient than in tokens but is harder to get working [▶ 24:32](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1472); memories and skills are an early, still manual, form of learning from experience [▶ 26:36](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1596); scaling RL needs very stable infrastructure, and verifiable problems run out quickly because easy ones stop carrying signal [▶ 28:12](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1692) [▶ 29:15](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1755); and long-running agents still get stuck on a wrong idea, partly from misunderstanding under-specified problems [▶ 30:46](https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o?t=1846).

**How it connects to this week.** The talk is the language-model side of the lecture's second half: test-time compute as a scaling axis, RL that makes long reasoning emerge, and verification as the limiting factor. His point that easy problems stop carrying signal is the third toy's zero-advantage groups seen from the other end.

## Solved problems {#problems}

**1. How many samples?** One sample of a planner succeeds with probability 0.05. With a perfect verifier, how many samples give a 90% chance of success? What if $p = 0.005$?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Solve $1 - (1 - p)^N \ge 0.9$ (@eq-bestofn): $N \ge \ln 0.1 / \ln(1 - p)$. For $p = 0.05$: $N \ge 2.303 / 0.0513 = 44.9$, so 45 samples. For $p = 0.005$: $N \ge 2.303 / 0.00501 = 459.4$, so 460. Ten times rarer success costs ten times the compute: for small $p$, $N \approx 2.3/p$.
:::

**2. A group with one right answer.** A GRPO group of 8 has exactly one right answer. Compute the advantages. What happens to the update if the group size doubles to 16 with still exactly one right answer?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Mean $1/8 = 0.125$; population standard deviation $\sqrt{0.125 \cdot 0.875} = 0.331$. Right answer: $(1 - 0.125)/0.331 = 2.646$; wrong ones: $-0.125/0.331 = -0.378$ (@eq-grpo-adv). With 16: mean $0.0625$, sd $\sqrt{0.0625 \cdot 0.9375} = 0.242$, so $+3.873$ and $-0.258$. The rarer the success within its group, the larger its advantage: normalization by the standard deviation makes a single success count heavily, which is what lets GRPO learn from problems it rarely solves.
:::

**3. Elo and model size.** By the rule of thumb of 120 Elo per doubling, how much larger would a model need to be to gain 600 Elo? What is the weaker side's win chance across that gap?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$600/120 = 5$ doublings: $2^5 = 32$ times larger. Win chance (@eq-elo): $1/(1 + 10^{600/400}) = 1/(1 + 31.6) = 3.1\%$.
:::

**4. Which recipe?** You have robot demonstrations and reasoning labels, the robot needs 10 Hz control, and you want operators to be able to correct the robot by editing its plan. ECoT, reasoning dropout or reasoning pre-training?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Pre-training cannot write reasoning at test time at all, so it offers nothing to edit. Full ECoT can be corrected but writes its reasoning at every step, which ran at about 1 Hz in the paper. Reasoning dropout is the natural fit: fast without reasoning, and able to switch it on. But check that its actions really depend on the reasoning when it is on: in this page's toy the dropout network ignored edits (@fig-edit). A practical compromise is to run fast without reasoning and reason only occasionally, or at a higher level, as in the two-level design (@fig-twolevel).
:::

**5. Rewarding the answer only.** A vision model answers a multiple-choice question about an image with 4 options. With grounded reasoning it is right 80% of the time; with hallucinated reasoning, which is cheaper to produce, it guesses from patterns and is right 60% of the time. The reward is 1 for a right answer. A second reward adds 0.5 when the objects mentioned in the reasoning are really in the image. What does each reward favour?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Answer only: expected reward 0.8 against 0.6, so grounded reasoning is preferred, but the gap is only 0.2, and on questions where pattern matching does as well as reasoning there is no pressure at all to ground the reasoning. With the extra check: grounded $0.8 + 0.5 = 1.3$, hallucinated $0.6 + 0 = 0.6$: the gap more than triples and holds on every question, which is the argument for verifying the reasoning as well as the answer (the lecture's ARGOS).
:::

## Recap

1. **A policy can reason before it acts.** ECoT writes plans, subtasks, motions and grounded features before its actions, with labels made by foundation models. *Why it matters:* it improved a VLA's generalization by 28 points without new robot data, and made it readable and correctable.
2. **Most of the gain comes from learning to produce the reasoning.** Reasoning pre-training and dropout keep much of it with no reasoning at test time; meaningless thinking tokens do not. *Why it matters:* robots need fast control; in the toy, reasoning labels cut the demos needed for 75% success from about 2\,795 to 1\,830.
3. **Reasoning that is not used cannot be edited.** *Why it matters:* interpretability and human correction require actions that actually depend on the reasoning; in the toy, the faster dropout network ignored edits.
4. **Test-time compute is a scaling axis, bounded by the model and the verifier.** Search beats a bigger model on problems the model sometimes solves, and loses on those it almost never does; an imperfect verifier caps what search can reach. *Why it matters:* it decides where to spend compute, in training or at inference.
5. **RL can make reasoning emerge, once the base model is good enough.** GRPO's group-relative advantages reward whatever works, including thinking longer; groups with no success teach nothing. *Why it matters:* it is how today's reasoning models are trained, and why they appeared only once base models were strong.
6. **Verify the reasoning, not just the answer.** *Why it matters:* in vision and robotics, right answers from hallucinated reasoning are common, and outcome-only rewards reinforce them.

## Cheat sheet & connections

| Name | Formula | Where |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Embodied CoT | $-\log p(c \mid o, \ell) - \log p(a \mid o, \ell, c)$ | @eq-ecot |
| Reasoning dropout | as ECoT, with $m \cdot c$, $m \sim$ Bernoulli$(1 - p_{\text{drop}})$ | @eq-dropout |
| Best-of-N | $1 - (1 - p)^N$ | @eq-bestofn |
| Learned verifier | $\arg\max_i V(o, a_i)$; capped by $V$'s own errors | @eq-verifier |
| Elo ★ | $1/(1 + 10^{-\Delta/400})$; 120 Elo ≈ one doubling | @eq-elo |
| Reasoning as depth | sequential steps $\approx L (T + 1)$ | @eq-depth |
| Group advantage | $(r_i - \text{mean}\, r)/\text{std}\, r$ | @eq-grpo-adv |
| GRPO ★ | clipped ratio × $\hat A$, minus $\beta$ KL to $\pi_{\text{ref}}$ | @eq-grpo |
| Learning signal | $1 - (1 - p)^G$ | @eq-signal |

**Common pitfalls.**

- Writing reasoning at every control step and losing the control rate.
- Assuming the reasoning explains the behaviour: check that actions change when the reasoning does.
- Expecting meaningless extra tokens to substitute for meaningful reasoning labels.
- Spending test-time compute on problems the model essentially never solves.
- Trusting a learned verifier at large $N$: search finds its blind spots.
- Starting RL from a base model with near-zero success, or on problems it always solves.
- Rewarding only final answers when right answers can come from hallucinated reasoning.

**Connections.** [Week 5](w05-reinforcement-learning-2.qmd)'s PPO is GRPO's parent; the critic is replaced by a group average. [Week 7](w07-sequence-modeling-transformers.qmd)'s transformers and tokens are why reasoning can simply be more tokens. [Week 8](w08-world-models.qmd)'s planning with learned models is test-time compute for control. [Week 9](w09-generalist-robot-policies.qmd)'s VLAs are what ECoT makes reason, and π0.5's subtask prediction is reasoning at the high level of @fig-twolevel. [Week 11](w11-frontiers.qmd) looks at what is still missing. In the ADM Companion: [Monte Carlo tree search]({{< var adm_url >}}/part2/ch09-online-planning.html#monte-carlo-tree-search), the search behind AlphaGo.

